# Langmuir Production Management — Workspace Guide

This workspace centralizes Brendan's production systems so they can be edited
and deployed from one place. Each system stays in its own git repo (so Railway
only restarts the service that actually changed) but they all live as siblings
under this folder.

## Scope every request first — this folder is the REAL system

**This folder is Langmuir's live, real production system.** Real customer
names, real work-order data, real Railway services on the `langmuir-*`
GitHub org. Treat everything in it as sensitive and internal.

It is easy to conflate this with **Finn Operations**, Brendan's separate
consulting/showroom brand (finnoperations.com), because Finn Operations is
*built from* this system — it's an anonymized, SpaceX-styled clone with
generic names ("Production Line 1") and "ghost functionality" for demoing
capabilities to prospects and investors. But the showroom, the private
business-planning app (ops-hub), and client-specific pitch demos (e.g. DYC)
all live in a **different, separate folder** ("Anonymized ERP Clone") that is
**not mounted in this workspace**. If it isn't mounted here, Claude cannot
read or edit it in this session.

Before starting any request in this project, triage which world it's in:

- **Real Langmuir ops** (this folder) — changes to `pms/`, `pms-test/`,
  `ci/`, `hub/`, `procurement/`, `tooling/`, `scheduling/`, `vision/`,
  `google-scripts/`, or anything touching real production/inventory data.
  Proceed as documented below.
- **Finn Operations showroom, ops-hub, or a client demo** (MRP module, DYC
  quoting tool, `/pricing`, `/results`, hub tours, Railway service
  `brendan-showroom`, repo `ShowroomPMS`/`FinnOpsHub`) — this needs the
  "Anonymized ERP Clone" folder, not this one. If that folder isn't
  connected in the current session, say so and ask Brendan to connect it
  (or open the request in that project) rather than guessing or trying to
  recreate showroom logic here.
- **Ambiguous** ("update the production board," "add a module") — ask which
  world before touching files. Getting this wrong risks either editing the
  wrong (live) system or leaking real Langmuir data/branding into a public
  demo.

**Hard rule:** never copy real customer names, real work-order/production
data, or Langmuir branding out of this folder into anything showroom- or
demo-related. The anonymization has to happen deliberately, not by reusing
real files as a shortcut.

## Current state — read SYSTEM-STATE.md first

`SYSTEM-STATE.md` (workspace root) is the ground-truth map of which repo/service
serves each feature, verified 2026-07-23. **pms is the center of gravity —
modules keep migrating into it** (KPI board and cycle count both did). Before
editing any feature, confirm which repo serves it now; retired snapshots live
in `_archive/` and must not be edited expecting production changes.

## Layout (reorganized 2026-07-23 — see SYSTEM-STATE.md)

```
.
├── pms/                  # LangmuirPMS — the operator system (Railway: langmuir-pms)
│                         #   includes /kpi (KPI board) and /cyclecount — both absorbed from
│                         #   their former standalone services
├── pms-test/             # LangmuirPMS_Test — staging clone (Railway: langmuir-pms-test)
├── ci/                   # langmuir-ci — CI/support-intake boards (Railway: langmuir-ci)
├── hub/                  # langmuirhub — landing portal linking every tool (Railway: langmuir-hub)
├── procurement/          # Langmuir-procurement — supply-chain decision support (Railway: langmuir-procurement)
├── scheduling/           # LangmuirScheduling (Railway: langmuir-scheduling)
├── tooling/              # langmuir-tooling (Railway: langmuir-tooling; Sheet-backed)
├── vision/               # LangmuirVision — part ID by photo (Railway: langmuir-vision)
├── google-scripts/       # clasp-managed Apps Scripts: pms-locations (LIVE inventory ledger),
│   └── <script-name>/    #   tooling-sheet
├── scripts/              # helper scripts: config.sh is the single source of truth
├── techsupport/          # Epicor API / access reference docs
├── Apollo First Production Run Revisions/   # active product drawings
├── _archive/             # retired systems + completed analyses (see _archive/README.md)
├── _to_delete/           # staged for deletion
├── SYSTEM-STATE.md       # ground-truth service map — keep updated when anything migrates
└── CLAUDE.md             # this file
```

## How Claude should use this workspace

**Important:** The sandbox where Claude runs shell commands can read and write
files in this folder, but can't reliably run `git commit`/`git push` or
`clasp pull/push` against the mount (deletes are blocked, which breaks git's
lock-file dance). So the workflow is:

- **Claude edits files** directly in the relevant subfolder using Read/Write/Edit.
- **Brendan runs the helper scripts from his Mac Terminal** — Claude proposes
  the exact command, Brendan pastes it.

Standard flow when Brendan asks for a change:

1. Edit files in the relevant subfolder (`pms/`, `ci/`, `hub/`, or
   `google-scripts/<name>/` — check SYSTEM-STATE.md if unsure which serves
   the feature).
2. Show Brendan the diff and wait for approval.
3. On approval, tell him the exact command to run in Terminal:
   ```
   cd ~/Documents/Claude/Projects/Langmuir\ Production\ Management\ System
   ./scripts/sync-repo.sh pms push "fix: corrected line totals"
   ```
   Or for a Google Script:
   ```
   ./scripts/sync-gscript.sh pms-locations push
   ```

Before editing, it's usually worth pulling latest — same idea, ask Brendan to
run from Terminal:
- `./scripts/sync-repo.sh pms pull`
- `./scripts/sync-gscript.sh pms-locations pull`

To see what's changed across everything: `./scripts/status.sh`

When a change spans several repos at once (a shared style, a logo, a header
tweak), don't hand Brendan one command per repo — point him at
`./scripts/push-all.sh`, which walks every repo in `config.sh` that has pending
work and delegates each push to `push.sh`:

```
./scripts/push-all.sh -n                              # dry run: preview every repo
./scripts/push-all.sh -m "fix: langmuir torch mark"   # confirm each repo, y/n/a/q
./scripts/push-all.sh -y -m "msg" pms hub ci          # no prompts, only these repos
```

It commits with `git add -A`, so tell him to run `-n` first — if a repo has
unrelated work in flight, that repo should be pushed on its own with
`./scripts/push.sh <repo> "its own message"`.

If Brendan asks Claude to "try it from the sandbox," Claude can attempt
`git status` (read-only) which works, but should not attempt commits or pushes
from the sandbox.

## Adding a new system

- **New GitHub repo:** add a line to `REPOS=( ... )` in `scripts/config.sh`,
  then run `./scripts/bootstrap.sh`.
- **New Google Script:** find its Script ID (script.google.com → Project Settings),
  add a line to `GSCRIPTS=( ... )` in `scripts/config.sh`, then run
  `./scripts/bootstrap.sh`.

## Prerequisites (one-time setup on the host machine)

- `git` — already installed on macOS.
- `clasp` for Google Scripts: `npm install -g @google/clasp`, then `clasp login`.
- Git auth: work repos authenticate to the **Langmuir GitHub account** through a
  dedicated SSH identity. `~/.ssh/config` defines a `github-langmuir` host alias
  (work key `id_ed25519_langmuir`, `brendan@langmuirsystems.com`); the default
  `github.com` host stays on the personal/school key. So every work remote is
  `git@github-langmuir:BrendanLangmuir/<repo>.git` (already set in
  `scripts/config.sh`), and each work clone runs
  `git config user.email "brendan@langmuirsystems.com"` so commits are attributed
  to the work account. If a clone still points at `git@github.com:…`, repoint it
  with `git remote set-url origin git@github-langmuir:BrendanLangmuir/<repo>.git`.

## Important deployment notes

- **The KPI board and cycle count are pms pages now** (`/kpi` since
  2026-07-21, `/cyclecount` via the Postgres engine) — their old standalone
  repos are retired in `_archive/`. The separate-repo principle still applies
  to the remaining services: a commit to one repo only restarts that service.
- **Apps Script bound to a Sheet/Doc** is fine for clasp — the script ID is
  what matters, not whether it's standalone. After `clasp push`, the new code
  is live in that Sheet immediately; there's no separate deploy step unless
  the script is published as a web app or add-on.
- **Never commit secrets.** If a script needs an API key, use Apps Script
  Properties Service (Project Settings → Script Properties) rather than
  hardcoding it.
